      "text": "```\nMONTEVERDI'S L'ORFEO (1607) — TECHNICAL ANALYSIS\n---------------------------------------------------\nThe libretto: by Alessandro Striggio.\nFive acts; the Orpheus myth."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-OPERA.md#009",
      "path": [
        "07-OPERA.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:theater-performance:07-opera, git-history:theater-performance:07-opera]",
        "canonical_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "concepts": "[opera]",
        "current_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "id": "maxim:theater-performance:opera",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "theater-performance",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[opera]",
        "section": "theater-performance",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Opera",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "MUSICAL INNOVATIONS:\n  Word painting: when Orpheus sings of dying, the\n    music descends; when he sings of the lyre, a lyre\n    sounds. The music depicts what the text describes.\n  Stile concitato (\"excited style\"): rapid, repeated\n    notes for agitation — Monteverdi's invention.\n    The first systematic musical emotional rhetoric.\n  Orchestra: ~36 instruments (huge for the period).\n    Different instrument groups for different realms:\n    - Strings and flutes = the earthly pastoral world\n    - Brass and trombones = the underworld (Hades)\n    The instrument palette is the first systematic\n    use of orchestral color for dramatic meaning.\n    Compare: Wagner's Leitmotif system is this idea\n    taken to its logical extreme."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-OPERA.md#010",
      "path": [
        "07-OPERA.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:theater-performance:07-opera, git-history:theater-performance:07-opera]",
        "canonical_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "concepts": "[opera]",
        "current_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "id": "maxim:theater-performance:opera",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "theater-performance",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[opera]",
        "section": "theater-performance",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Opera",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE PROBLEM OPERA DEFINES:\n  When text and music compete, which wins?\n  Monteverdi: music must serve the text (Camerata ideal).\n  But: when a great singer has a great aria, the text\n  is subordinated to the singer's display of virtuosity.\n  This tension drives the entire history of opera.\n```"
    },
