      "text": "### Mozart: The Synthesis"
    },
    {
      "id": "07-OPERA.md#028",
      "path": [
        "07-OPERA.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:theater-performance:07-opera, git-history:theater-performance:07-opera]",
        "canonical_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "concepts": "[opera]",
        "current_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "id": "maxim:theater-performance:opera",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "theater-performance",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[opera]",
        "section": "theater-performance",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Opera",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nMOZART'S OPERAS\n---------------\nDon Giovanni (1787): opera buffa but with a serious ending.\nLe nozze di Figaro (1786): comic opera as political comedy.\nCosi fan tutte (1790): comedy about fidelity and illusion.\nDie Zauberflöte (1791): singspiel (spoken dialogue + music)."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-OPERA.md#029",
      "path": [
        "07-OPERA.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:theater-performance:07-opera, git-history:theater-performance:07-opera]",
        "canonical_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "concepts": "[opera]",
        "current_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "id": "maxim:theater-performance:opera",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "theater-performance",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[opera]",
        "section": "theater-performance",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Opera",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "MOZART'S ACHIEVEMENT:\n  Character in music: each character in Don Giovanni has\n  a distinctive musical language. Don Giovanni's music\n  is seductive, mercurial, unstoppable. Donna Elvira's\n  is overwrought, passionate. Leporello's is comic and\n  practical. The characters SOUND different, not just\n  act different."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-OPERA.md#030",
      "path": [
        "07-OPERA.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:theater-performance:07-opera, git-history:theater-performance:07-opera]",
        "canonical_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "concepts": "[opera]",
        "current_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "id": "maxim:theater-performance:opera",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "theater-performance",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[opera]",
        "section": "theater-performance",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Opera",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE ENSEMBLE: Mozart was the master of the ensemble\n  — scenes where multiple characters sing simultaneously,\n  each with their own text and emotional state.\n  The Act II finale of Figaro is 20 minutes of continuous\n  music in which the plot complications multiply and\n  resolve. Multiple characters with multiple agendas\n  sing simultaneously in musical counterpoint.\n  Musical structure = dramatic structure."
    },
    {
      "id": "07-OPERA.md#031",
      "path": [
        "07-OPERA.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:theater-performance:07-opera, git-history:theater-performance:07-opera]",
        "canonical_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "concepts": "[opera]",
        "current_path": "theater-performance/07-OPERA.md",
        "id": "maxim:theater-performance:opera",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "theater-performance",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[opera]",
        "section": "theater-performance",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Opera",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "This is the opposite of Opera Seria (one character,\n  one aria) — it is musical-dramatic integration.\n```"
    },
